<article class="env-definition" id="07BI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07BI">Definition <span data-tag="07BI">10.18.1</span>.</a> A <em>local ring</em> is a ring with exactly one maximal ideal. If $R$ is a local ring, then the maximal ideal is often denoted $\mathfrak m_ R$ and the field $R/\mathfrak m_ R$ is called the <em>residue field</em> of the local ring $R$. We often say “let $(R, \mathfrak m)$ be a local ring” or “let $(R, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a local ring” to indicate that $R$ is local, $\mathfrak m$ is its unique maximal ideal and $\kappa = R/\mathfrak m$ is its residue field. A <em>local homomorphism of local rings</em> is a ring map $\varphi : R \to S$ such that $R$ and $S$ are local rings and such that $\varphi (\mathfrak m_ R) \subset \mathfrak m_ S$. If it is given that $R$ and $S$ are local rings, then the phrase “<em>local ring map $\varphi : R \to S$</em>” means that $\varphi $ is a local homomorphism of local rings. </p>
